# Outbound communication — the owner signs it, so the owner sends it

Applies whenever text leaves the building with the founder's name on it: email to a supplier, clinic,
dietitian, investor or tester; pitch decks; landing copy; app-store text.

## Never press Send

Draft, format, verify — then **stop and hand it over**. No Send, no Submit, no Publish, no "reply
all", even when the user says "send it"; that means *prepare it to be sent*. Email cannot be
recalled: Gmail's Undo Send is a few seconds, and after that the only options are living with it or
an awkward second email. **A silently wrong sentence in a sent email costs more than a phone
round-trip** (`model-budget.mdc`) — spend the extra read.

## Every claim traces to a source

Same rule as `product-truth.mdc`, tighter: an outbound sentence about what the company *is* or *does*
must be quotable from `website/`, `investor-pov/`, `marketing/`, or shipped code. Do not add a third
clause to a sentence because it sounds like a startup. Do not upgrade "supervised protocol" to
"trial", "pilot" to "partnership", or "one patient" to "our patients".

## Clinical claims

| Never | Instead |
|---|---|
| Call an n=1 supervised protocol a *trial*, *study*, or *clinical validation* | "a six-month supervised protocol", "one patient under follow-up" |
| Attribute an outcome to one ingredient | Name it as one component of a full protocol, and say so |
| Send patient name, ID, DOB, contact, or a raw export | Anonymized chart or aggregate only — no identifiers in the image either |
| Imply the app diagnosed, treated, or prescribed | The clinician's protocol did; the app measured and kept the loop |

If the recipient is a supplier or partner, they will screenshot whatever you write. Assume it ends up
on their website.

## One language per letter

Do not mix Hebrew and English in the same message; pick the recipient's language and stay there
(`language-policy.mdc`). Hebrew copy must read like a person wrote it, not like a translation
(`localize-speak-like-a-person.mdc`). Always-English glossary still applies: brand, units, `CGM`,
device names.

Right-to-left bodies: email addresses, URLs and numbers keep `dir="ltr"` inside Hebrew prose, or the
domain paints to the wrong side and looks like corrupted data.

## Gmail mechanics that have already bitten us

- **Plain-text mode silently drops inline images.** Before pasting a formatted letter, switch the
  compose window to **rich text**. A letter sent from plain-text mode arrives with the charts gone.
- **Also attach the image with the paperclip**, not only inline — inline images get stripped by some
  clients and by forwarding.
- **Browser automation cannot paste an image** into Gmail: `navigator.clipboard.read()` throws
  `NotAllowedError: Document is not focused` in the agent-driven tab, and Gmail's body is a
  Trusted-Types-protected contenteditable, so `innerHTML` assignment fails. Build the letter as a
  standalone HTML file, have the owner copy-paste it, and let the owner drop the image.
- Compose in a background tab may render the "taking longer than usual" shell — poll
  `document.title` for `Compose Mail` before trusting a snapshot.
- Check the **To** chip and subject after any programmatic edit; a `?to=&su=` URL is not proof the
  fields stuck.

## Before handing it back

Say plainly what is ready and what is still the owner's call. Flag anything you could not verify —
a link you did not open, a number you could not source, a claim that depends on their judgment.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] Draft only — nothing sent, and said so?
- [ ] Every product claim traceable; no invented capability or third clause?
- [ ] Clinical language proportional to the evidence (protocol ≠ trial)?
- [ ] No patient identifiers in the text **or** the attached image?
- [ ] One language, correct `dir` on emails/URLs?
- [ ] Rich text confirmed, image attached as well as inline?
- [ ] Brand written **HEALTHINGS.AI**?
